    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-15774" textLabel="UDP-arabinopyranose mutase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O82705" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="275" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">The |FRAME: G-12117 UAM1| gene of |FRAME: TAX-4530 &lt;i&gt;Oryza sativa&lt;/i&gt;| encodes one of three |FRAME: MONOMER-15774 &quot;UDP-arabinopyranose mutase&quot;| enzymes in that plant |CITS: [17182701]|. The gene was cloned and expressed in |FRAME: TAX-7108 &lt;i&gt;Spodoptera frugiperda&lt;/i&gt;| Sf9 insect cells and purified to homogeneity |CITS: [20057139]|. The mutase activity of the recombinant protein was determined using |FRAME: UDP-L-arabinofuranose UDP-L-arabinofuranose| or |FRAME: UDP-L-arabinopyranose UDP-L-arabinopyranose| as substrates. The ratio of the pyranose to furanose forms reached a plateau at 93:7 from both pyranose- and furanose-forming directions. Native rice mutase is believed to exist as a complex composed of several different UAM monomers. A combination of all three forms resulted in about 2-fold increase in activity, suggesting that UAM isozymes work synergistically to increase activity |CITS: [20057139]|.</Comment>
